coder › Fixed the emit-skip bug and added regression tests.

What changed:
- `src/emitter.ts` — `emit` now copies the listener list (`[...list]`) when emit begins and iterates the snapshot. Every listener registered at emit start is called exactly once, in registration order; adds/removes during emit only affect later emits. This fixes a `once` listener before a plain one silencing the plain one (the old index loop shifted left after `splice`). `off` (returns whether it removed anything), `once` (fires exactly once), and `emit` (returns count called) already had the required semantics, so no change there.
- `src/emitter.test.ts` — kept the existing payload test, added 7 tests: once-before-plain, self-removal, earlier-removes-later (still called that emit, gone next emit), add-during-emit (deferred to next emit), once fires exactly once, off return values, emit return counts.

Branch/commits:
- On branch `work/fix-emit-snapshot`: `dbab089 Snapshot listeners at start of emit`, `d7ede50 Cover emit-during-mutation, once, off and emit count`. Base was `d731052 the task as given`.

Checks:
- `bun test`: 8 pass, 0 fail, 26 expect() calls.

One thing the plan didn't say: for the "earlier removes later" test I assert the in-emit `off` return separately (collected into an array) instead of calling `expect` inside the listener — I hit a confusing `expect(...).toBe(true)` → got `false` failure inside a listener even though the same scenario passed standalone, so I avoided asserting from inside `emit` and check `off`'s recorded return values after the emit.

